# Security & Encryption Standards

## No Clear-Text Secrets
- **Zero tolerance** for clear-text passwords, API keys, or secrets in any file
- Sensitive values must be fetched from a **secure vault** (Azure Key Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, etc.) or encrypted using **AES CBC** encryption
- When a secure vault provider is used, AES encryption and `secureProperties` in `mule-artifact.json` are **not required** — the vault handles secret protection at runtime

## Encryption Key Storage (when AES encryption is used instead of vault)
- Encryption keys must **never** appear in properties files or source code
- Acceptable locations: VM arguments in Studio Run Configuration (`-M-Dxxxx=123123`)
- In production: secure vault (Azure Key Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, etc.)
- Do not create shared run configurations that expose keys

## Secure Properties Declaration
- If sensitive properties are fetched via a secure vault provider (e.g., `${azure-key-vault-properties-provider::secret::...}`), declaring them in `mule-artifact.json` `secureProperties` is **not required**
- If sensitive properties use AES-encrypted values instead of a vault, they **must** be declared as secured properties in `mule-artifact.json`
- Examples of properties that must be protected (via vault or encryption): client IDs, client secrets, passwords, encryption keys, certificate passwords

## Client ID Enforcement
- Apply Client ID enforcement policy (preferably as Automated Policy)
- Use HTTP Basic Authentication Header option for flexibility
- Each API calling another API should have its own client application defined
- Non-production environments may use a shared client application for convenience

## Secure Vault Integration
- Use Azure Key Vault (or equivalent) for managing secrets at runtime
- Reference secrets via secure property providers, not inline values
- Secrets in the vault follow the project naming convention (e.g., `sfl-consumer-key`, `mule-mq-app-client-id`)

## Logging & Data Protection
- All logging **must** use the Insulet Custom Logger Connector (`module-logger`), never the built-in Mule `logger`
- **Never log raw payloads** — use masked (`build-log-with-masked-payload`) or encrypted (`build-log-with-encrypted-payload`) operations only
- `sensitiveKeyParts` must be configured in the custom logger global config to mask PII fields (e.g., `ssn,password,secret,token,email`)
- `safeKeys` must be configured for false-positive exclusions (e.g., `passwordPolicy`)
- The logger `encryptionKey` must be sourced from secure vault — never hardcoded
- `entityDetails` must contain **only** non-sensitive business identifiers (e.g., `orderId`)
- PROD: When a single `log4j2.xml` is used, consider filtering/deployment config to set `com.insulet.*` categories to `WARN`; `DEBUG` only during active triage and **must be reverted**

## Review Checklist
- [ ] No plain-text passwords, keys, or secrets in any file
- [ ] Sensitive properties fetched from secure vault (e.g., Azure Key Vault) — or, if AES-encrypted, declared in `mule-artifact.json` `secureProperties`
- [ ] Encryption keys not stored in source-controlled files
- [ ] Secure vault integration configured for all environments
- [ ] Client ID enforcement configured for API-to-API calls
- [ ] No raw payload logging — only masked or encrypted operations used
- [ ] `sensitiveKeyParts` configured with all PII/credential field fragments
- [ ] Logger `encryptionKey` sourced from secure vault, not hardcoded
- [ ] When PROD requires it, `com.insulet.*` categories are at WARN or ERROR
